title: Twelve, Thirteen, Fourteen

    I have a confession.

    Three pebbles in my satchel have been renamed. They are now called Twelve, Thirteen and Fourteen.

    I believe you finished the isotopes lesson recently. You will see why.

reply: carbon-12, 13 and 14?? -> chat-hal-pebbles-carbon
reply: that's so nerdy -> chat-hal-pebbles-nerdy

node chat-hal-pebbles-nerdy
kind: reply
pose: proud

    Yes. It is the highest compliment I know.

    But there is a point to it. Look at them. All three pebbles look the same to me. Same shape. Same colour. And one of them, I cannot say which, is slightly heavier.

reply: so they're like isotopes -> chat-hal-pebbles-carbon
reply: which one is the heavy one -> chat-hal-pebbles-heavy

node chat-hal-pebbles-carbon
kind: reply
pose: explain

    Exactly so. Carbon-12, carbon-13, carbon-14.

    All three are carbon. Six protons each. The protons make it carbon, and the protons do not change.

    What changes is the neutrons. Six, seven, eight. That is the entire difference between my pebbles.

reply: and 14 is the unstable one, right? -> chat-hal-pebbles-unstable
reply: ok but which pebble is which lol -> chat-hal-pebbles-heavy

node chat-hal-pebbles-heavy
kind: reply
pose: sorry

    I do not know.

    I put them in a bag together on Monday and I have lost the labels.

    This is why I do not recommend naming things Twelve, Thirteen and Fourteen. They do not answer to their names. Neither does Tuesday.

reply: oh Hal -> chat-hal-pebbles-unstable
reply: ok but what about the real atoms -> chat-hal-pebbles-unstable

node chat-hal-pebbles-unstable
kind: reply
pose: explain

    Ah. Fourteen. Yes.

    It is the unstable one. An unstable nucleus can change, and when it does it gives off radiation.

    But note this, because it matters: isotope does not mean radioactive. Carbon-12 and carbon-13 are perfectly stable. Only some isotopes change.

